Peer review in open source software allows a larger community of developers to scrutinize the code, which can uncover vulnerabilities that might be missed by a smaller team.
The collaborative development model of open source software leads to quicker patching of security flaws, as multiple contributors can work on solutions simultaneously.
Open source software has a proven track record of security, with many widely used systems like Linux and Apache being considered highly secure due to their open nature.
Transparency in open source software reduces the risk of hidden backdoors or malicious code, as anyone can inspect the source code for potential issues.
The collective intelligence of the open source community leads to more robust and secure software over time, as diverse perspectives contribute to better security practices.